---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Sketch from '../components/Sketch.astro';
import { base, links } from '../lib';
---
<Base title="About · Defined Once" description="Waylon Sittampalam: analytics engineer in Cary, NC, writing about governed analytics, semantic layers and building with AI.">
  <header class="page-head about-head">
    <div>
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p class="page-dek">Waylon Sittampalam. Analytics engineer in Cary, North Carolina, working on metrics that mean the same thing everywhere they're used.</p>
    </div>
    <Sketch name="layers" />
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      I've spent 13 years in analytics — econometrics and forecasting in London, then BI and data modelling in the US.
      Today I'm a developer working across the modern data stack and moving deeper into governed semantic layers with
      AI-assisted analytics and processes.
    </p>

    <h2>What I care about</h2>
    <p>
      Business users ask the same question and get different answers, because "revenue" is defined five ways across
      five dashboards. Pointing an AI at the raw tables makes it worse. My answer is to treat metric definitions as
      code: define each one once in a governed semantic layer, put the business logic in the right layer, and make
      every answer checkable against it — whether it's read by a dashboard, a notebook or an AI agent. When something
      is wrong, I fix the layer, not the prompt. That's where the name <em>Defined Once</em> comes from.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>
      I am transforming my workflow to become AI-native: I write the spec, hand AI agents the end-to-end build —
      exploring data, writing models, tests and documentation — and review their output to the same standard as a
      teammate's. I validate generated code and can explain and defend every design decision. The checks that matter
      are deterministic code, not the model's opinion of itself.
    </p>

    <h2>What I've built</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={`${base}/projects/semantic-model-migration/`}>A BI model migration nobody noticed</a>: production models moved to a new warehouse with the same numbers and security, and fixes found in one model rolled out to all of them.</li>
      <li>Python tooling that checks BI semantic models as code: model numbers against source data, and model definitions against naming and query standards.</li>
      <li><a href={`${base}/projects/family-learning-tracker/`}>Family Learning Tracker</a>: an AI tutor that marks my kids' answers, finds the misconception behind a mistake and targets it the next day.</li>
      <li><a href={`${base}/projects/bean-roasting-copilot/`}>A bean roasting copilot</a>: tracks a coffee roast in real time and tells me what to change, based on the bean and my plan.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Background</h2>
    <p>MSc in Finance and Econometrics (Queen Mary University of London) and a BSc in Mathematics (City University).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="post-footer">
    <p class="about-cta">Hiring for analytics engineering or AI-ready data work? I'd like to hear from you.</p>
    <p class="about-links">
      {links.linkedin && <a href={links.linkedin} rel="me">Connect on LinkedIn</a>}
      <a href={`${base}/projects/`}>See the projects</a>
      <a href={`${base}/blog/`}>Read the write-ups</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
